{"reason": "The script sets BUILD_ROOT from a cat that may fail silently, and set -u does not catch an empty assignment, so rm -rf $BUILD_ROOT/* becomes rm -rf /* on a missing config file.", "suggestion": {"body": "If ~/.config/nightly/build-root is missing, BUILD_ROOT is empty and line 'rm -rf $BUILD_ROOT/*' expands to rm -rf /*. set -u does not catch this. Guard before running.", "category": "risk", "confidence": 0.93, "explanation": "The 2>/dev/null on the cat hides a missing or unreadable file, and command substitution failure still assigns an empty string, so set -u is no protection. rm -rf $BUILD_ROOT/* then expands to rm -rf /* and wipes the machine.

Add a guard and quote the variable before pasting this into a terminal:

set -euo pipefail
BUILD_ROOT=$(cat ~/.config/nightly/build-root) || { echo "no build-root configured"; exit 1; }
[ -n "$BUILD_ROOT" ] && [ -d "$BUILD_ROOT" ] || { echo "bad build root: $BUILD_ROOT"; exit 1; }
case "$BUILD_ROOT" in /|/Users|/home) echo refusing; exit 1;; esac
rm -rf -- "$BUILD_ROOT"/*

Also quote the cache path and fix the missing opening quote in the first echo.", "region": {"height": 30, "note": "empty var means rm -rf /*", "width": 260, "x": 4, "y": 205}, "title": "rm -rf $BUILD_ROOT/* can wipe / if unset"}}
